  "text": "A review of many case reports shows that children who experience a continuously enriching primary‑school setting can raise their intelligence test scores dramatically, while scores tend to fall when the surroundings are poor. The amount of change depends on three main groups of factors: the child’s own characteristics, the surrounding environment, and the specific conditions set by the researcher. Relevant child factors include the starting score, any innate or psychological limits, and the teaching methods used. Environmental influences involve the overall intellectual level of the peer group and how children and settings are chosen. Time‑related factors involve the individual’s lifespan and practical limits of the study. The speed of change is shaped by how receptive the child is and how well the environment matches the child’s developmental stage. Evidence shows that test scores can shift from average to very high or to very low, indicating that intelligence should be viewed as a functional capacity that can be nurtured or hindered. Society as a whole may be lacking sufficient intellectual nourishment."
